solution:
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) {
    throw new Error("Can't divide by zero");
  }
  return a / b;
}

function safeDivide(a, b) {
  try {
    // If divide throws, JavaScript jumps straight to catch.
    return divide(a, b);
  } catch (e) {
    // e is the Error object: its text is in e.message.
    return e.message;
  }
}

console.log(safeDivide(10, 2));
console.log(safeDivide(1, 0));

alternatives:
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) {
    throw new Error("Can't divide by zero");
  }
  return a / b;
}

function safeDivide(a, b) {
  let result;
  try {
    result = divide(a, b);
  } catch (error) {
    result = error.message;
  }
  return result;
}

console.log(safeDivide(10, 2));
console.log(safeDivide(1, 0));

statement.en:
Write a function `checkAge(age)` that:
- throws a `TypeError` with the message `"Age must be a number"` if `age` is not a number;
- throws a `RangeError` with the message `"Age must be between 0 and 120"` if `age` is less than 0 or greater than 120;
- otherwise, returns `"Valid age"`.
The starter code has a function `test(age)` that calls `checkAge` and returns its result or, if it throws, `e.name + ": " + e.message`. Don't change it: the tests use it.
Example: `test(-1)` returns `"RangeError: Age must be between 0 and 120"`.

statement.es:
Escribe una función `checkAge(age)` que:
- lance un `TypeError` con el mensaje `"Age must be a number"` si `age` no es un número;
- lance un `RangeError` con el mensaje `"Age must be between 0 and 120"` si `age` es menor que 0 o mayor que 120;
- en otro caso, devuelva `"Valid age"`.
El código de partida trae una función `test(age)` que llama a `checkAge` y devuelve su resultado o, si lanza un error, `e.name + ": " + e.message`. No la cambies: los tests la usan.
Ejemplo: `test(-1)` devuelve `"RangeError: Age must be between 0 and 120"`.

starter:
function checkAge(age) {
  // your code here
}

// Don't change this function: the tests use it.
function test(age) {
  try {
    return checkAge(age);
  } catch (e) {
    return e.name + ": " + e.message;
  }
}

console.log(test(30));
console.log(test(-1));

solution:
function checkAge(age) {
  // Check the type first: "200" > 120 is true,
  // so a text would otherwise give a RangeError.
  if (typeof age !== "number") {
    throw new TypeError("Age must be a number");
  }
  if (age < 0 || age > 120) {
    throw new RangeError("Age must be between 0 and 120");
  }
  return "Valid age";
}

// Don't change this function: the tests use it.
function test(age) {
  try {
    return checkAge(age);
  } catch (e) {
    return e.name + ": " + e.message;
  }
}

console.log(test(30));
console.log(test(-1));

alternatives:
function checkAge(age) {
  if (typeof age !== "number") throw new TypeError("Age must be a number");
  if (age >= 0 && age <= 120) return "Valid age";
  throw new RangeError("Age must be between 0 and 120");
}

// Don't change this function: the tests use it.
function test(age) {
  try {
    return checkAge(age);
  } catch (e) {
    return e.name + ": " + e.message;
  }
}

console.log(test(30));
console.log(test(-1));

